-- title:
--   Diamond operators act faithfully on reduced q-expansions
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field, let $M$ be a nonzero natural number whose image in $K$ is nonzero, and let $H$ be a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$. Let $\gamma \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ lie in $\Gamma_0(M)$, and write $\langle\gamma\rangle :=$ [`CohCarrier.gamma0Units M ⟨γ, hγ⟩`](def/CohCarrier_Level.html#L121) for the unit of $\mathbb{Z}/M$ obtained by reducing the lower-right entry of $\gamma$ (a unit, with inverse the reduction of the upper-left entry); assume $\langle\gamma\rangle$ does not lie in the subgroup $H \sqcup \langle -1\rangle$ of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ generated by $H$ together with $-1$. Then there are a weight $k \in \mathbb{Z}$, four modular forms $f, g, f_1, g_1$ of weight $k$ on the subgroup of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$ determined by $\Gamma_H(M)$ (the image in $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ of the elements of $\Gamma_0(M)$ whose associated unit lies in $H$), power series $p_f, p_g, p_{f_1}, p_{g_1} \in \mathbb{Z}[[q]]$, and a nonzero $c \in \mathbb{C}$ such that: each of $p_f, p_g, p_{f_1}, p_{g_1}$ becomes, after mapping its coefficients into $\mathbb{C}$, the $q$-expansion of width $1$ of the corresponding form; $f_1 = c\,(f\mid_k \gamma)$ and $g_1 = c\,(g\mid_k \gamma)$ as functions on the upper half-plane; and, writing $\bar p$ for the image of $p$ in $K((q))$ under coefficientwise reduction to $K$, one has $\bar p_g \neq 0$, $\bar p_{g_1} \neq 0$ and $\bar p_f \, \bar p_{g_1} \neq \bar p_g \, \bar p_{f_1}$.
--
--   The conclusion exhibits an explicit witness to the fact that the diamond operator $\langle d \rangle$ attached to $\gamma$ moves the reduction modulo the characteristic of $K$ of the modular function $f/g$: the cross-ratio inequality says precisely that $\bar p_f/\bar p_g$ and $\bar p_{f_1}/\bar p_{g_1}$ differ in $K((q))$. It is used to prove that the action of $\Gamma_0(M)/\pm\Gamma_H(M)$ on the $q$-expansion function field of $X_H(M)$ in residue characteristic prime to $M$ is faithful, the input to Igusa's irreducibility statement for the reduction of $X_H(M) \to X_0(M)$, and is cited by the full-level results producing the corresponding group homomorphism into algebra automorphisms.
-- source:
